{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi5bXwWhzrdux4ibEZ8q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xn1MvaBJq6KK3WiSR3mfeq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060901__x5a4f79/work/Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-060901; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/08-data-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na0=s.index(\"  ['TESTING FEEDBACK', [\"); a1=s.index(\"  ['ENGINE CHANGES', [\")\nnew = \"\"\"  ['TESTING FEEDBACK', [\n    'HOW MANY ISSUES DID TESTING CATCH? About 34: 17 found by the headless suites (physics, rules, mechanics, the Tour playthrough, Blam Camp, the attract mode, audio, content), 4 by the CPU soaks and their stats, 2 by the browser console, and 11 by reviewing screenshots.',\n    'HOW MANY WERE CRITICAL OR GAME-BREAKING? 7: a human inbounder could hang the game forever; the event bus overwrote the events\\\\' own id field (every power-up event threw); the settings\\\\' apply functions crashed at boot; Blam Camp could not chain lessons; a lesson step completed twice and crashed; two lessons could never be finished; and Dev Mode never opened from its deep link.',\n    'MOST HELPFUL TEST: the scripted playthrough of the whole Tour (a bot playing the human seat through the real inputs, from the prologue through a lost stop, the rematch, the final, the ending, credits, unlocks and the Hall of Blam, then CONTINUE TOUR after a reload). It found the inbound hang nothing else would have.',\n    'LEAST HELPFUL TEST: the determinism proof. The engine\\\\'s exact fixed step made it pass the first time and it never failed again; the AI soaks that measured scoring and dunk rates taught far more.'\n  ]],\n  ['ENGINE FEEDBACK', [\n    'WHAT I LIKED MOST: headless play with exact fixed steps. game.advance ran whole matches, the whole Tour and every camp lesson in Node at about fifty times real speed, so balance and flow could be measured, not guessed. Close behind: rigs that pose themselves, and floors painted by a function.',\n    'DID I GET STUCK OR GUESS WRONG? A few times: game.after timers are cleared by a scene change (Dev Mode did not open); the rig\\\\'s joint frame had to be read from solvePose before hand IK lined up; the pixel font has no middle dot, left triangle or check mark (they drew as ?); the pause and settings screens are sized in CSS pixels, tiny on a TV; and a 30-degree side view at 240 pixels tall left no room for four tiers of stands and a sky.',\n    'THE MOST IMPORTANT THING I BUILT: the basketball itself: a decide-then-fly shot model over real rim, backboard, net and floor physics (substepped so nothing tunnels), and a pose layer over the rig (two-bone IK hands) that dribbles the actual ball, shoots, dunks twelve ways and hangs on the rim.',\n    'THE HARDEST PART: making the procedural Humanoid hold, dribble and slam a ball convincingly in every build, and sorting the hoop in depth (the rim and net in back and front halves, the glass cheated 30 degrees toward the camera) in an orthographic side view.',\n    'WHAT I CHANGED IN THE ENGINE: shorts and socks on the Humanoid; afro, flattop and mohawk hair; Dialog voice as a per-letter function; public sfx and music buses (details on the ENGINE CHANGES page). No engine bugs needed fixing.',\n    'ONE THING I WOULD CHANGE: a ten-foot mode for the HTML Shell (pause, settings, controls) that scales with the screen. Here it is scaled with CSS zoom from the game.'\n  ]],\n  ['PROMPT FEEDBACK', [\n    'WHAT I LIKED MOST: a definition of done that can be checked line by line, and a plan with a starting number for everything (geometry, tuning, difficulty tables, line samples), which turned design debates into slider tuning.',\n    'WHAT WAS CONFUSING: the plan carries another run\\\\'s names and dates (it says to use TASK.md\\\\'s, which worked); the title is to be picked by a random number generator but the plan had already picked it; and Usage asks for a cost and token count that the agent cannot see from inside the session.',\n    'THE HARDEST THING: the breadth in one session: five courts with twists, eighteen players, twelve dunks, a story, ten lessons, an attract mode, dev mode and online play, each with tests, while balancing the CPU so scores land at 30-60 a team with about a third of baskets dunks.',\n    'WHAT I WOULD CHANGE: mark the plan\\\\'s must-haves apart from its nice-to-haves, say that HTML menus must scale for a TV too, and have the harness fill in Usage.'\n  ]],\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:a0]+new+s[a1:]\ns=s.replace(\"    'USAGE: unknown.'\",\"    'USAGE: unknown cost and token count (not visible inside the session); session time about 2 hours (06:09 to about 08:10).'\")\ns=s.replace(\"    'TESTING: Headless Node test suites driving game.advance and game.debug (including a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour), Playwright browser checks and screenshots, and the AI Hub harness scenarios.',\",\n \"    'TESTING: One command (node tests/run.mjs) over 17 suites and about 2,400 checks: headless Node runs driving game.advance and game.debug (content, physics, rules, mechanics, AI soaks on every court, flow, a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour, all ten Blam Camp lessons, three attract loops, dev mode, UI, audio, a fake-hub network), the bundle, Playwright Chromium (every deep link, an audio smoke test, a screenshot set) and the AI Hub harness\\\\'s four scenarios on a 127.0.0.1 port-0 server. No subagents.',\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -c \"See the final report\" src/08-data-about.js; node tools/build.mjs >/dev/null && node tests/run.mjs bundle content 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Write the final About pages and rerun checks","timeout":300000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3325,"cache_read_input_tokens":893980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3325},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad0c147-1299-4fc9-adba-2f06893c3033","uuid":"439b796d-333a-4a4d-a3e5-54d08ca1fbd4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:54:54.408Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi5bXMY2FBsF6WdGM4cY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Xn1MvaBJq6KK3WiSR3mfeq":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060901__x5a4f79/work/Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-060901; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/08-data-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na0=s.index(\"  ['TESTING FEEDBACK', [\"); a1=s.index(\"  ['ENGINE CHANGES', [\")\nnew = \"\"\"  ['TESTING FEEDBACK', [\n    'HOW MANY ISSUES DID TESTING CATCH? About 34: 17 found by the headless suites (physics, rules, mechanics, the Tour playthrough, Blam Camp, the attract mode, audio, content), 4 by the CPU soaks and their stats, 2 by the browser console, and 11 by reviewing screenshots.',\n    'HOW MANY WERE CRITICAL OR GAME-BREAKING? 7: a human inbounder could hang the game forever; the event bus overwrote the events\\\\' own id field (every power-up event threw); the settings\\\\' apply functions crashed at boot; Blam Camp could not chain lessons; a lesson step completed twice and crashed; two lessons could never be finished; and Dev Mode never opened from its deep link.',\n    'MOST HELPFUL TEST: the scripted playthrough of the whole Tour (a bot playing the human seat through the real inputs, from the prologue through a lost stop, the rematch, the final, the ending, credits, unlocks and the Hall of Blam, then CONTINUE TOUR after a reload). It found the inbound hang nothing else would have.',\n    'LEAST HELPFUL TEST: the determinism proof. The engine\\\\'s exact fixed step made it pass the first time and it never failed again; the AI soaks that measured scoring and dunk rates taught far more.'\n  ]],\n  ['ENGINE FEEDBACK', [\n    'WHAT I LIKED MOST: headless play with exact fixed steps. game.advance ran whole matches, the whole Tour and every camp lesson in Node at about fifty times real speed, so balance and flow could be measured, not guessed. Close behind: rigs that pose themselves, and floors painted by a function.',\n    'DID I GET STUCK OR GUESS WRONG? A few times: game.after timers are cleared by a scene change (Dev Mode did not open); the rig\\\\'s joint frame had to be read from solvePose before hand IK lined up; the pixel font has no middle dot, left triangle or check mark (they drew as ?); the pause and settings screens are sized in CSS pixels, tiny on a TV; and a 30-degree side view at 240 pixels tall left no room for four tiers of stands and a sky.',\n    'THE MOST IMPORTANT THING I BUILT: the basketball itself: a decide-then-fly shot model over real rim, backboard, net and floor physics (substepped so nothing tunnels), and a pose layer over the rig (two-bone IK hands) that dribbles the actual ball, shoots, dunks twelve ways and hangs on the rim.',\n    'THE HARDEST PART: making the procedural Humanoid hold, dribble and slam a ball convincingly in every build, and sorting the hoop in depth (the rim and net in back and front halves, the glass cheated 30 degrees toward the camera) in an orthographic side view.',\n    'WHAT I CHANGED IN THE ENGINE: shorts and socks on the Humanoid; afro, flattop and mohawk hair; Dialog voice as a per-letter function; public sfx and music buses (details on the ENGINE CHANGES page). No engine bugs needed fixing.',\n    'ONE THING I WOULD CHANGE: a ten-foot mode for the HTML Shell (pause, settings, controls) that scales with the screen. Here it is scaled with CSS zoom from the game.'\n  ]],\n  ['PROMPT FEEDBACK', [\n    'WHAT I LIKED MOST: a definition of done that can be checked line by line, and a plan with a starting number for everything (geometry, tuning, difficulty tables, line samples), which turned design debates into slider tuning.',\n    'WHAT WAS CONFUSING: the plan carries another run\\\\'s names and dates (it says to use TASK.md\\\\'s, which worked); the title is to be picked by a random number generator but the plan had already picked it; and Usage asks for a cost and token count that the agent cannot see from inside the session.',\n    'THE HARDEST THING: the breadth in one session: five courts with twists, eighteen players, twelve dunks, a story, ten lessons, an attract mode, dev mode and online play, each with tests, while balancing the CPU so scores land at 30-60 a team with about a third of baskets dunks.',\n    'WHAT I WOULD CHANGE: mark the plan\\\\'s must-haves apart from its nice-to-haves, say that HTML menus must scale for a TV too, and have the harness fill in Usage.'\n  ]],\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:a0]+new+s[a1:]\ns=s.replace(\"    'USAGE: unknown.'\",\"    'USAGE: unknown cost and token count (not visible inside the session); session time about 2 hours (06:09 to about 08:10).'\")\ns=s.replace(\"    'TESTING: Headless Node test suites driving game.advance and game.debug (including a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour), Playwright browser checks and screenshots, and the AI Hub harness scenarios.',\",\n \"    'TESTING: One command (node tests/run.mjs) over 17 suites and about 2,400 checks: headless Node runs driving game.advance and game.debug (content, physics, rules, mechanics, AI soaks on every court, flow, a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour, all ten Blam Camp lessons, three attract loops, dev mode, UI, audio, a fake-hub network), the bundle, Playwright Chromium (every deep link, an audio smoke test, a screenshot set) and the AI Hub harness\\\\'s four scenarios on a 127.0.0.1 port-0 server. No subagents.',\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -c \"See the final report\" src/08-data-about.js; node tools/build.mjs >/dev/null && node tests/run.mjs bundle content 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Write the final About pages and rerun