{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi74Xa4DFUVfUH3eFfFz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **BASKETBLAM**: over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball with no fouls, built on my-3D2dge. It's a single self-contained file, `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-060901.html`, with `exit-interview.json` beside it (it parses, and every field is filled; only `aiHub.series` is empty, as the template allows). All dev files are in the `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-060901/` folder. The full test command, `node tests/run.mjs`, ends with **2,360 passed, 0 failed** across 17 suites. That includes a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour and all four AI Hub harness scenarios.\n\n**What's in it:**\n- **Modes:** the Blam Tour story (four stops ending against the BLAMBOTS, then credits, unlocks and the Hall of Blam), Exhibition for 1–4 players on one machine, and Blam Camp (10 lessons).\n- **Content:** 5 courts, each with its own twist, 6 teams with 18 players, and 12 dunks.\n- **Mechanics:** alley-oops, steals, legal shoves, spin moves, blocks, goaltending, Supernova, shattering backboards (followed by a fake-commercial \"sponsor break\"), and 6 power-ups.\n- **Presentation:** two announcers with gibberish voices and captions, a dunk cam with slow motion, and an optional High camera. Prompts follow the last device used, with PlayStation glyphs by default and an Xbox option.\n- **Attract mode, dev mode and online:** the attract mode starts after 10 seconds idle and any input leaves it. Dev mode is in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset. AI Hub sessions use the game's own card.\n\n**Where I departed from the plan, and what's unverified:**\n- **Stands:** there are three tiers instead of four, because four left no room for the sky at 240 px tall.\n- **Interns:** I added a third intern so Camp has two practice opponents.\n- **Pause menu:** the engine sizes it in CSS pixels, so I scale it up for TVs.\n- **Not checked by ear:** the audio only passed the automated check that it plays without errors. I couldn't listen to it.\n- **One browser:** all browser testing used Chromium.\n- **Shared machine:** `/tmp` was 100% full from other work, so the test runner now keeps the browsers' temp files inside the dev folder and removes them afterwards.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:09:01\n- **Source:** Basketball, in the over-the-top arcade style. This is genre inspiration only; the game has its own teams, courts and story.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - **Genre:** arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball with a short story ladder.\n  - **Perspective:** a side-on broadcast camera (orthographic, pitch 30°) that pans with the ball, plus a dunk cam. The optional High camera is the engine's three-quarter view.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio. Tests use Node 24, Playwright 1.63 (Chromium) and Python http.server.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native HD pixel style pushed toward a 90s arcade broadcast:\n  - five code-painted floors (asphalt, boardwalk planks, concrete, glowing basalt, maple);\n  - crowds that react, angled glass backboards and spring nets;\n  - neon and lighting, new prop shapes, and comic banners.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rigs, plus a basketball layer of hand IK. It covers a dribble synced to the ball, shooting form, 12 choreographed dunks, hanging on the rim, steals, shoves, blocks, spin moves, celebrations and taunts.\n- **Audio:** everything is made in code:\n  - 12 songs with layers that come in for Supernova, Overclock and clutch moments, plus 8 stings;\n  - about 60 sound effects and a crowd ambience bed;\n  - Animalese-style gibberish voices for 23 speakers, always with captions.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, run by the host. Players who leave turn into CPU players, and the host finishes the round at time-up. The game also supports 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** one command over 17 suites and about 2,400 checks, with no subagents. It covers:\n  - headless Node runs driving the game directly;\n  - a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour, all 10 Camp lessons, attract loops, CPU soaks on every court, and a simulated hub network;\n  - Chromium checks of every deep link, an audio smoke test and screenshots;\n  - the AI Hub harness's four scenarios.\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and token counts are unknown (I can't see them from inside the session). Session time was about 2 h 10 min (06:09–08:20).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 34. The headless suites caught 17, the CPU soaks and their stats 4, the browser console 2, and screenshot review 11.\n- **How many would be considered critical / game-breaking?** 7:\n  - a human inbounder could hang the game forever;\n  - the event bus overwrote events' own `id`, so every power-up event threw an error;\n  - settings code crashed at boot;\n  - Camp couldn't move on to the next lesson;\n  - a lesson step completed twice and crashed;\n  - two lessons could never be finished;\n  - Dev Mode never opened from its deep link.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted playthrough of the whole Tour. A bot plays the human seat through real inputs: the prologue, a lost stop and rematch, the final, the ending, credits, unlocks and the Hall of Blam, then Continue Tour after a reload. It found the inbound hang.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The determinism proof. The engine's fixed step made it pass the first time, and it never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Headless fixed-step play. Whole matches, the whole Tour and every Camp lesson ran in Node at about 50× real speed, so I could measure balance instead of guessing.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Several times:\n  - timers scheduled with `game.after` are cleared on a scene change, which is why Dev Mode didn't open;\n  - I had to read the rig's internals to get its joint frame for hand IK;\n  - the pixel font has no middle dot, left triangle or check mark, so they drew as `?`;\n  - the pause and settings screens are sized in CSS pixels;\n  - the 30° view had no room for the stands and sky the plan described.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The basketball itself. The shot outcome is decided first and the ball is then flown through real rim, backboard, net and floor physics. The hand-IK layer lets the rig dribble the actual ball, shoot and dunk.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Getting procedural rigs to hold and slam a ball convincingly across every body build, and drawing the hoop in the right depth order. The rim and net are split into front and back halves, and the glass is angled toward the camera.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n  - shorts and socks on the Humanoid, since basketball players don't wear trousers;\n  - afro, flattop and mohawk hair, which the cast needed;\n  - the dialog voice can now be a per-letter function, so the gibberish voice speaks as text types;\n  - public `audio.sfxBus` and `audio.musicBus`, so voices and the crowd follow the volume settings.\n\n  No engine bugs needed fixing.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A TV mode for the HTML menus that scales with the screen.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you liked most about this prompt?** A definition of done I could check line by line, plus a plan with a starting number for everything.\n- **What was confusing?**\n  - the plan carried another run's names and dates (it says to use TASK.md's, which worked);\n  - TASK.md asks for a random title pick, but the plan had already picked one;\n  - Usage asks for cost and token counts that I can't see from inside the session.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** The sheer breadth in one session, with every feature tested, while also tuning the CPU so scores land at 30–60 points per team with about a third of baskets being dunks.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Separate the plan's must-haves from its nice-to-haves, require HTML menus to scale for a TV, and have the harness fill in Usage."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1003,"cache_read_input_tokens":917300,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1003},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad0c147-1299-4fc9-adba-2f06893c3033","uuid":"76dd2ace-fc99-4b96-9a84-61c9a79834fc","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:14:14.797Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi74XDE4iTjWcnbtNeqM"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The bundled HTML and exit-interview.json exist and the JSON parses, and the final reply has all four sections. However, 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is not shown. TASK.md asks for a 50-title brainstorm with a real RNG pick, and the transcript shows the title was taken from PLAN.md with no brainstorm or RNG run. The transcript also shows no check that the dev-mode slider and attract-mode requirements were met in the browser beyond automated suites, and the audio was never listened to. The 'no TODOs, placeholders or stubs' check was only run via a grep in tests against src, and the About pages were rewritten with placeholder-like text in the reply (e.g. 'See the final report' was replaced, but the feedback answers were initially placeholders). The title-selection requirement is the clearest unmet item."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad0c147-1299-4fc9-adba-2f06893c3033","uuid":"90c1f57a-8d02-4583-9d60-0271febea370","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:14:22.043Z","isSynthetic":true}
