{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi7kywB2reW4izbqSsgP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I checked the four points the stop hook raised. One remains a limit I can't close: I still can't hear the audio. The full test command still ends with 2,360 passed, 0 failed, and `exit-interview.json` still parses.\n\n- **Title pick:** TASK.md's 50-title brainstorm and real RNG pick were done when the plan was written. PLAN.md §1 records \"fifty riffs on 'basketball' were brainstormed… Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 18\" and says \"Do not re-pick it.\" STAGE.md tells me to use the plan's title, and the two files don't conflict. I kept BASKETBLAM and added that provenance to the Source Information page on the in-game About screen.\n- **Dev mode and attract mode in a real browser:** I drove the shipped file in Chromium with real key presses, using `tools/verify-browser.mjs`:\n  - After 11 s idle on the title the attract mode started, and one key press returned to the title.\n  - I reached a match through the menus by keyboard. Escape paused it, and the pause menu lists Resume, How to Play, Restart Game, Dev Mode, Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit.\n  - Dev Mode opened the panel. Twenty presses moved the Run speed slider from 108 to 128, and the player then ran 128 units in a second instead of 102.\n  - Reset All put every slider back to its default, and Escape closed the panel. There were no page errors.\n- **Audio:** I still can't listen to it, so I measured the game's actual output in Chromium with `tools/verify-audio.mjs`:\n  - The music plays and drops to silence when stopped.\n  - Each voice has its own pitch character, the slam is low, and the shattering glass is bright.\n  - The voices were about 10 dB quieter than the music. I raised them, and gave the robot voice extra level because its effect halves it. All three voices now sit around 0.019 RMS against music at about 0.035.\n  - A recording to listen to is at `check-output/audio-sample.webm`.\n- **No TODOs, placeholders or stubs:** a scan of the game code (`build/game.js`, inlined verbatim in the shipped file) and its source finds none, and the shipped file contains no TODO or FIXME anywhere, engine included. The About pages now carry the final feedback text; the \"See the final report\" stand-ins were replaced before the earlier final reply, and a bundle test fails if they ever come back.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:09:01\n- **Source:** basketball, in the over-the-top arcade style. This is genre inspiration only; the game has its own teams, courts and story.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md. The title BASKETBLAM was picked there from 50 brainstormed riffs with `secrets.randbelow(50)` → 18.\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball with a short story ladder, Exhibition for 1–4 players, and the Blam Camp tutorial.\n  - Perspective: a side-on broadcast camera (orthographic, pitch 30°) that pans with the ball, a dunk cam with slow motion, and an optional High camera (the engine's three-quarter view).\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio. Building and testing used Node 24, Playwright 1.63 with Chromium, and Python http.server.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native HD pixel style pushed toward a 90s arcade broadcast:\n  - five code-painted floors (asphalt, boardwalk planks, concrete, glowing basalt, maple);\n  - crowds that react, angled glass backboards and spring nets;\n  - neon and canvas lighting, new prop shapes, and comic banners.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rigs, plus a basketball layer of two-bone IK hands. It covers a dribble synced to the ball with crossovers, shooting form, 12 choreographed dunks, hanging on the rim, steals, shoves, blocks, spin moves, celebrations and per-player taunts.\n- **Audio:** everything is made in code:\n  - 12 songs with layers that come in for Supernova, Overclock and clutch moments, plus 8 stings;\n  - about 60 sound effects and a crowd ambience bed;\n  - Animalese-style gibberish voices for 23 speakers, always with captions.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, using the game's own card. The host runs the game and sends snapshots; other players send their inputs. Players who leave become CPU players, a copy with no player can host, and the host finishes the round at time-up. The game also supports 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** one command over 17 suites and about 2,400 checks, with no subagents. It covers:\n  - headless Node runs driving the game directly;\n  - a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour, all 10 Camp lessons, three attract loops, CPU games on every court, and a simulated hub network;\n  - the bundle file itself;\n  - Chromium checks of every deep link, an audio smoke test and screenshots;\n  - the AI Hub harness's four scenarios.\n\n  The real-key browser check and the audio measurement scripts are kept in `tools/`.\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and token counts are unknown (I can't see them from inside the session). Session time was about 2 h 10 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 34. The headless suites caught 17, the CPU-game stats 4, the browser console 2, and screenshot review 11. The real-key browser and audio checks then caught one more: the voices were too quiet under the music.\n- **How many would be considered critical / game-breaking?** 7:\n  - a human inbounder could hang the game forever;\n  - the event bus overwrote events' own `id`, so every power-up event threw an error;\n  - settings code crashed at boot;\n  - Camp couldn't move on to the next lesson;\n  - a lesson step completed twice and crashed;\n  - two lessons could never be finished;\n  - Dev Mode never opened from its deep link.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted playthrough of the whole Tour. A bot plays the human seat through real inputs, through a lost stop and rematch, the final, the ending, credits, unlocks and the Hall of Blam, then Continue Tour after a reload. It found the inbound hang.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The determinism proof. The engine's fixed step made it pass the first time, and it never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Headless fixed-step play. Whole matches, the Tour and every Camp lesson ran in Node at about 50× real speed, so I could measure balance instead of guessing.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Several times:\n  - timers scheduled with `game.after` are cleared on a scene change;\n  - I had to read the rig's internals to get its joint frame for hand IK;\n  - the font has no middle dot, left triangle or check mark;\n  - the HTML menus are sized in CSS pixels;\n  - the 30° view had no room for four tiers of stands and a sky at 240 px tall.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The basketball itself. The shot outcome is decided first and the ball is then flown through real rim, backboard, net and floor physics, with sub-steps so nothing passes through the rim. The hand-IK layer dribbles the actual ball, shoots and dunks.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Getting procedural rigs to hold, dribble and slam a ball convincingly across every body build, and drawing the hoop in the right depth order in an orthographic side view.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n  - shorts and socks on the Humanoid;\n  - afro, flattop and mohawk hair;\n  - the dialog voice can now be a per-letter function;\n  - public `audio.sfxBus` and `audio.musicBus`.\n\n  These were needed for basketball outfits, the cast, voices that speak as text types, and volume control of the voices and crowd. No engine bugs needed fixing.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A TV mode for the HTML menus that scales with the screen.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** A definition of done I could check line by line, plus a plan with a starting number for everything.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan carried another run's names and dates. TASK.md asks for a random title pick, but the plan had already made one and said not to redo it. Usage asks for cost and token counts that I can't see from inside the session.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** The breadth in one session, with every feature tested, while tuning the CPU to land at 30–60 points per team with about a third of baskets being dunks.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Separate the plan's must-haves from its nice-to-haves, require HTML menus to scale for a TV, say explicitly whether the stage should redo the title pick or keep the plan's, and have the harness fill in Usage."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1113,"cache_read_input_tokens":934776,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1113},"output_tokens":726,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad0c147-1299-4fc9-adba-2f06893c3033","uuid":"c78f6b48-8c86-4587-99c0-37da4d862198","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:23:18.156Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi7kwg176hUffghb6LqT"}
