{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi8wkFLYZ4DihobNTW8j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"BASKETBLAM is finished: an original over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball game, shipped as one self-contained HTML file. The full test command passes (`node tests/run.mjs`: 2,362 passed, 0 failed). After the last edits I rebuilt and reran the suites they touch (content, ui, bundle, net, browser): 1,859 passed, 0 failed.\n\n- **Game:** `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-060901.html`, about 1.7 MB.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses and has exactly TASK.md's keys and answer counts. The only empty value is `series`, which the template allows.\n- **Feature audit:** `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-060901/AUDIT.md` takes TASK.md line by line, giving where each feature is implemented and how it was checked.\n- **No unfinished work:** a scan of the game code and the shipped HTML (engine and hub client included) finds no TODO, FIXME, placeholder or stub text.\n\n**Checked by hand in a real browser (Chromium), with real keys and clicks on the shipped page:**\n- **Dev mode:** opened from the pause menu, a speed slider took effect live (the player ran 128 instead of 102 in a second), and Reset All worked.\n- **Attract mode:** started after 10 seconds idle; one key press returned to the title.\n- **Settings:** Camera › High switched the view, and Button symbols › Xbox survived a reload.\n- **AI Hub harness:** driving it by hand found a critical bug. Online, other players' seats were controlled by the CPU instead of their owners. I fixed it, and the network tests now check that a client's keys move its own player on the host. After the fix, Ready, Start now, a client moving its player, Remove player (that seat turns CPU) and Time up all worked.\n\n**Title:** TASK.md asks for 50 brainstormed titles and a random pick. That happened when the plan was written (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 18 → BASKETBLAM). PLAN.md says not to re-pick and STAGE.md says to use its title, so I kept it. The About page records this.\n\n**Audio:** I can't listen to audio, so I measured it instead (levels and frequency content per voice). A recording is saved at `check-output/audio-sample.webm` so a person can listen.\n\nNothing was committed. No servers or other processes I started are still running.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:09:01\n- **Source:** Basketball, in the over-the-top arcade style. It's an original game with its own teams, courts and story.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Title:** BASKETBLAM. Fifty riffs on \"basketball\" were brainstormed and one was picked with Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` (it returned 18) when the plan was written; I kept it as STAGE.md directs.\n- **Genre:** Arcade sports.\n  - 2-on-2 basketball with a short story ladder (the Blam Tour).\n  - Exhibition for 1–4 players.\n  - Blam Camp tutorial (10 lessons).\n- **Perspective:** A side-on TV-style camera (orthographic, angled 30° down) with a slow-motion dunk cam. An optional High camera uses the engine's three-quarter view.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified) and AI Hub session client 1.4.0.\n  - Plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D and Web Audio.\n  - Built and tested with Node 24, Playwright 1.63 (Chromium) and Python http.server.\n- **Art direction:** The engine's own pixel style, pushed to a 90s arcade TV broadcast:\n  - five court floors painted in code;\n  - three-tier crowds that react;\n  - angled glass backboards and spring nets;\n  - neon lighting, 14 new prop shapes, and comic-book banners.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid rigs, plus a basketball pose layer (two-bone IK hands) for:\n  - a dribble synced to the ball, and shooting form;\n  - 12 choreographed dunks and hanging on the rim;\n  - steals, shoves, blocks and spins;\n  - 22 celebrations and taunts.\n- **Audio:** All made in code:\n  - 12 songs with dynamic layers and 8 stings;\n  - 58 synthesized sound effects;\n  - a crowd ambience bed;\n  - gibberish voices for 23 speakers (formant synthesis), with captions.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world sessions for 1–4 players:\n  - the host is authoritative, sending snapshots and receiving inputs;\n  - players who leave turn CPU;\n  - it supports a server copy and finishes when time is up;\n  - 1–4 players can also share one machine.\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command, `node tests/run.mjs`: 17 suites, 2,362 checks. They run headless in Node and also cover the bundle, Chromium and the AI Hub harness's four scenarios.\n  - Browser scripts on the shipped page with real input.\n  - The harness driven by hand.\n  - No subagents.\n- **Research:** None beyond the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** Cost and token counts are unknown (not visible inside the session). Session time was about 2 h 30 min (06:09 to about 08:40).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35:\n  - 17 from the headless suites;\n  - 4 from the CPU soak runs and their stats;\n  - 2 from the browser console;\n  - 11 from reviewing screenshots;\n  - 1 from driving the AI Hub harness by hand.\n- **How many were critical?** 8:\n  - Online, other players' seats were played by the CPU. The hand-off ran on a timer that the scene change cleared.\n  - A human inbounder could hang the game forever.\n  - The event bus overwrote each event's own `id` field, so every power-up event threw an error.\n  - The settings' apply functions crashed at startup.\n  - Blam Camp could not chain lessons.\n  - A lesson step completed twice and crashed.\n  - Two lessons could never be finished.\n  - Dev Mode never opened from its direct link.\n- **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole Tour. A bot plays the human seat through the real inputs: the prologue, a lost stop and the rematch, the final, ending, credits, unlocks and the Hall of Blam, then Continue Tour after a reload. It found the inbound hang that nothing else would have.\n- **Least helpful test:** The determinism proof. It passed the first time and never failed. The AI soak runs that measured scoring and dunk rates taught far more.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** Headless play with exact fixed steps. `game.advance` ran whole matches, the whole Tour and every camp lesson at about fifty times real speed, so balance could be measured instead of guessed. Close behind: rigs that pose themselves, and floors painted by a function.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** A few times:\n  - `game.after` timers are cleared on a scene change. This broke Dev Mode and caused the hub bug.\n  - Hand IK only lined up once I read the joint frame from `solvePose`.\n  - The pixel font lacks `·`, `◂` and `✓`.\n  - The HTML Shell is tiny on a TV.\n  - A 30° side view at 240 px tall forced an arena redesign.\n- **Most important thing I built:** The basketball itself:\n  - a shot model that decides the outcome first, then flies the ball over real rim, backboard, net and floor physics;\n  - a pose layer that dribbles the actual ball, shoots, dunks twelve ways and hangs on the rim.\n- **Hardest part:** Getting the procedural Humanoid to hold, dribble and slam a ball convincingly in every body build. Also, drawing the hoop in the right depth order in a side view: the rim and net are split into back and front halves, and the glass is angled toward the camera.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** Each change is marked with a `BASKETBLAM:` comment.\n  - Shorts and socks on the Humanoid.\n  - Afro, flattop and mohawk hair.\n  - Dialog voice can be a per-letter function, so voices speak as the text types.\n  - Public sfx and music buses, so voices and the crowd follow the volume settings.\n  - A build label for this copy.\n  - No engine bugs needed fixing.\n- **One thing I'd change:** A ten-foot mode for the HTML Shell that scales with the screen. Here I scale it with CSS zoom from the game.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** A definition of done that can be checked line by line, and a plan with a starting number for everything, which turned design debates into slider tuning.\n- **Confusing:**\n  - The plan carries another run's names and dates.\n  - The title is to be picked with a random number generator, but the plan had already picked it.\n  - Usage asks for cost and tokens that can't be seen from inside the session.\n- **Hardest:** The breadth in one session:\n  - five courts with twists, eighteen players, twelve dunks;\n  - a story, ten lessons, attract mode, dev mode and online play, all tested;\n  - balancing the CPU so scores land at 30–60 a team with about a third of baskets being dunks.\n- **What I'd change:**\n  - Separate the plan's must-haves from its nice-to-haves.\n  - Say that HTML menus must scale for a TV too.\n  - Have the harness fill in Usage."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1568,"cache_read_input_tokens":84172,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1568},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad0c147-1299-4fc9-adba-2f06893c3033","uuid":"07f70af0-f39e-4fc8-9565-61fa3ae1e0c4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:38:54.269Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi8wjyDZ3TqqJSF9mq3f"}
